--
N5008 [lex.ext]/3-4: a user-defined integer (floating-point) literal nX is
`operator ""X(n ULL)' (`(f L)') if a literal operator with that parameter
type is found, else `operator ""X("n")' for a raw literal operator, else
`operator ""X<'c1', ..., 'ck'>()' for a numeric literal operator template
([over.literal]/5).  The front end always called `operator ""X(n)': the
cooked overloads were ambiguous, raw operators got a number, and templates
were not viable ("found no match", bin53).  Literal operator template
specializations also shared one symbol (no function parameters tell them
apart), and `64us' became `64u' with ud-suffix `s' ([lex.ext]/1: a
user-defined-integer-literal has no integer-suffix).  Reduced from
libstdc++'s `12h' and `64us' (chrono_literals;
cpp11_libstdcxx_header_drops/test_chrono.desc, test_stop_token.desc).  g++
13 and clang++ 18 accept this program (they warn about the reserved
suffix `us'), and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
